# Library System Project Rules

This is a professional library management system.

Prioritize correctness, Clean Architecture, TDD, maintainability, testability, and a reproducible local setup.

## Informal user story

As a librarian, I register members and titles and lend up to three available titles per member in one loan, so the library never over-commits stock. As a member, I only see books I can actually borrow right now.

## Priorities

Prioritize:

- correctness of loan stock rules
- Clean Architecture (domain independent of EF and HTTP)
- clarity
- maintainability
- testability
- reproducible local setup
- developer-friendly commands
- professional UX without unnecessary complexity

## Avoid overengineering

Do not add:

- ASP.NET Identity UI
- refresh-token rotation or social login
- Redis, queues, or event sourcing
- Kubernetes
- client self-service loans, reservations, waitlists
- fines, due dates, or renewals
- global frontend state libraries
- MediatR or a generic repository unless it clearly simplifies this codebase

Keep the product focused and complete for catalog, member, and loan operations. Do not under-engineer the core workflow.

## Stack

Frontend:

- Angular (standalone components)
- TypeScript
- Angular Material
- HttpClient with a JWT interceptor
- unit tests for guards, API error parsing, and loan retry mapping

Use Angular Material for layout, tables, forms, dialogs, and feedback states. Use a black-and-green admin shell (header and sidebar). Create/edit in dialogs from an Add button; confirm deletes. Do not ship a plain list with an always-visible form.

Backend:

- ASP.NET Core Web API
- Clean Architecture: Domain, Application, Infrastructure, Api
- Entity Framework Core with MySQL (Pomelo)
- JWT Bearer authentication, roles `Admin` and `Client`
- Swagger UI for API exploration
- xUnit tests for domain, application, infrastructure, and API

Tooling:

- Docker for MySQL, backend, and frontend
- docker-compose for full-stack local execution
- justfile for common commands
- environment/config files for dynamic ports, connection strings, and API URLs

## Business rules

- Admin has full access (users, books, loans).
- Clients may only list books with available stock (`Quantity > 0`).
- Only an admin may create, list, or return loans.
- Each loan may include at most 3 distinct titles.
- Each loan may include only one unit of each title.
- A title may be loaned only when `Quantity >= 1`.
- A loan is persisted only when every selected title is available (atomic).
- If any title is unavailable, return `INSUFFICIENT_STOCK` with details so the admin can remove those titles and retry.
- Returning a loan restores available stock for each item, never above `TotalQuantity`.

`TotalQuantity` is how many copies the library owns. `Quantity` is how many copies are on the shelf now and is the value used for loan checks. Available copies cannot exceed total units.

## Command standards

Use a `justfile` as the main command interface.

Expected commands:

~~~bash
just install
just debug
just run
just run-backend
just run-frontend
just test
just test-backend
just test-frontend
just build
just docker-build
just docker-down
~~~

The README must document these commands.

Prefer `just` commands in documentation instead of long raw command chains, while still documenting underlying commands when useful.

## Docker standards

Dockerize everything:

- backend must have a Dockerfile
- frontend must have a Dockerfile
- root must have docker-compose.yml including MySQL with a healthcheck
- `docker compose up --build` should run the full application locally

The Docker setup should be simple and easy to run locally.

Do not introduce production-grade orchestration complexity.

## Environment and configuration standards

Use environment/config files for dynamic configuration.

Required files:

~~~txt
.env.example
backend/.env.example
frontend/.env.example
~~~

Do not commit real `.env` files.

Root `.env.example` should define shared Docker/local defaults such as:

~~~env
BACKEND_PORT=8080
FRONTEND_PORT=4200
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4200
SWAGGER_ENABLED=true
~~~

Backend configuration should support:

- API URLs/ports
- MySQL connection string
- JWT secret, issuer, audience, expiry
- allowed CORS origins
- Swagger enabled/disabled flag
- environment name

Frontend configuration should support:

- backend API base URL through a build-time environment file

Configuration should be documented in README.md.

## README documentation standard

Every meaningful change must be reflected in README.md.

The README must include:

- project overview and user story
- tech stack
- setup instructions
- environment variables
- how to install dependencies
- how to run backend
- how to run frontend
- how to run the full stack with Docker
- how to run tests
- how to use the justfile
- seeded credentials
- API examples
- Swagger/OpenAPI information
- error response examples
- design decisions
- assumptions
- AI usage summary linking to docs/AI_USAGE.md

If implementation behavior changes, update the README in the same change.

## API conventions

Version routes under `/api/v1`.

Anonymous:

- `POST /api/v1/auth/login`
- `GET /health`

Authorized:

- `GET /api/v1/auth/me`
- `GET /api/v1/books` (clients see available stock only)

Admin:

- user CRUD
- book CRUD
- loan create, list, return

Error envelope:

~~~json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "INSUFFICIENT_STOCK",
    "message": "One or more titles do not have enough available copies for this loan.",
    "details": [
      {
        "bookId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
        "title": "Dune",
        "available": 0
      }
    ]
  }
}
~~~

## Workflow

Before coding:

- create or update the plan
- keep scope small
- prefer simple implementation over clever implementation

During coding:

- make small changes
- write tests for loan policy before or with the implementation (TDD)
- run tests after meaningful changes
- update README when behavior, commands, configuration, or setup changes
- keep docs/AI_USAGE.md updated with concise prompts used

Before considering work complete:

- run backend tests
- run frontend tests
- run frontend build
- run Docker full-stack setup when Docker is available
- review README
- review Swagger
- run the principal-review skill

Do not claim something works unless tests/builds were executed.

All code, documentation, skills, UI copy, and seed data must be in English.
